{% extends 'help/_layout.html.twig' %}
{% block help_title %}Symptom Tracking{% endblock %}

{% block help_body %}
<figure class="figure d-block border rounded shadow-sm overflow-hidden mb-4 w-100">
    <img src="{{ asset('images/help/symptoms.png') }}" class="figure-img img-fluid mb-0 w-100"
         alt="The Symptom Tracking page with a daily 0–10 slider per symptom">
</figure>
<p>Symptom tracking closes the loop between what you eat and how you feel. You pick the symptoms that matter to
you, rate them daily, and {{ brand.name }} looks for connections with your diet.</p>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Getting started</h2>
<ol>
    <li>Open the <strong>Symptoms</strong> page.</li>
    <li>In <strong>Manage symptoms</strong>, turn on the ones you care about — joint pain, stiffness,
        bloating, headache, energy, mental clarity — or add your own.</li>
    <li>Each day, slide each symptom from 0 to 10. For "good" symptoms like energy, a higher number is
        better; for others, lower is better.</li>
</ol>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Reading the relationship charts</h2>
<p>Once you've logged a symptom for a few days, it gets its own tile on <strong>Insights &amp; Trends</strong>.
Tapping the tile opens a panel like this one for joint stiffness:</p>

<figure class="figure d-block border rounded shadow-sm overflow-hidden mb-4 w-100">
    <img src="{{ asset('images/help/symptoms-chart.png') }}" class="figure-img img-fluid mb-0 w-100"
         alt="A Trends relationship panel for joint stiffness, with small-multiples charts stacking the symptom against protein, carbs, fat, fiber, and inflammation score">
</figure>

<p>Each factor — diet macros, your <a href="{{ path('app_help_show', {topic: 'inflammation'}) }}">inflammation
score</a>, and (if you import them) health metrics like sleep or steps — gets its own compact chart in its
<strong>real units</strong>, stacked directly under the symptom. Rather than untangling several lines squashed
onto one shared, normalized axis, you scan straight down a column: does a spike in the symptom line up with a
spike — or a dip — just below it?</p>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">What "statistically significant" means</h2>
<p>Below each chart, {{ brand.name }} calls out only the factors worth your attention:</p>

<figure class="figure d-block border rounded shadow-sm overflow-hidden mb-4 w-100">
    <img src="{{ asset('images/help/symptoms-significance.png') }}" class="figure-img img-fluid mb-0 w-100"
         alt="The significant-correlations callouts under a joint stiffness panel, listing factors like protein and fiber with their r-values and day counts">
</figure>

<p>You don't need a statistics background to use this — here's the plain-language version of what's checked
before a factor makes that list:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Enough days to trust.</strong> There have to be at least 7 days where you logged both the
        symptom and the factor. One unusually good or bad day can't fake a pattern that needs a week or more of
        days lining up.</li>
    <li><strong>Not just a coincidence.</strong> {{ brand.name }} runs a standard statistical test that asks: "if there
        were really no connection here, how likely is it we'd still see a pattern this strong just by luck?"
        A factor only makes the list when that chance is under 5% — what statisticians call
        <strong>p &lt; 0.05</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <strong>r</strong> number next to each factor (like <code>r = 0.6</code>) says how tightly the two move
together, from <strong>−1</strong> (perfectly opposite — one goes up as the other goes down) through
<strong>0</strong> (no relationship) to <strong>+1</strong> (perfectly together). The closer to −1 or +1, the
stronger the pattern. And <strong>same-day vs. next-day</strong> just tells you when it showed up — sometimes
what you eat today doesn't affect a symptom until tomorrow, so {{ brand.name }} checks both.</p>
<p>One more thing worth remembering: these are <strong>descriptive, not causal</strong>. A significant
correlation is a good lead for your own experimenting — it doesn't prove that factor is <em>causing</em> the
symptom, and it isn't a diagnosis.</p>

<p class="text-muted small">Symptom tracking is optional and entirely opt-in — nothing shows up until you
enable a symptom, and a relationship panel needs a few logged days before it has anything to chart.</p>
{% endblock %}

